more than a dozen Democratic Democratic  presidential candidates have called for  the Attorney General to release the  Muller report one of those candidates  asking to see the entire report is  Senator Kamala Harris from the  California Democrat made that demand at  a campaign stop in Atlanta where she  also unveiled a new plan to increase pay  for public school teachers nationwide  now it's part of our road to 2020 she is  discussing the details only on CBS this  morning senator Harris joins us from  Washington senator good morning great to  have you on I want to get to your  education plan in just a moment but I do  want to begin with the news of day and  that is the Justice Department is  reversing its course now saying that the  entire Affordable Care Act should be  invalidated  what toll are all these legal battles on  the Affordable Care Act having on the  program itself and how will it sit with  voters so bianna when I was Attorney  General of California we defended the  Affordable Care Act and the need for its  existence listen I'm traveling around  the country these days as you know and  one of the the main things that keeps  people up at night that they worry about  is whether they're going to have access  to affordable health care and the  Affordable Care Act brought health care  to millions tens of millions and people  that otherwise didn't have it I feel  very strongly that supposedly ders  should stop playing politics with  people's public health people want to  know that pre-existing conditions will  not be a barrier to them receiving  mehelphelp care was a huge issue during  the midterms expecting the same in 2023  sure yes  you're right it's still an issue it is  what keeps people up at night and what  also keeps people up at night our wages  specifically for teachers and that's  something that you're talking about  today you are announcing that you have a  plan that will invest in American  teachers and give them a $13,500 raise  to close the teacher pay gap as you call  it that is a very lofty ambition and you  should be commended for it but the big  question is how are you gonna pay for it  sure well first of all you know I look  at my friends are successful in the  private sector and  I admire that they understand that we  really should look at these things  through the lens of whether it is a  we're going to get the return on the  investment so let's look at it as an  investment we can judge a society I  think always by the way it treats its  children and one of the greatest  expressions of love of our children is  that we invest in their education the  people who are going to educate our  children are our teachers and for too  long they have been paid substandard  wages and certainly not being paid their  value to us as a society and to our  children so I am proud of our initiative  that has been described as the largest  federal investment in closing the  teacher pay gap in history I think that  there is no question that we all know  there are two groups of people who are  raising our children it's our parents  which could be grandparents aunties and  uncles and it's our teachers so let's  play it pay them their value so how did  you come up with the figure of 13,500  and I'm still not sure how you're gonna  pay for it sure so we're gonna pay for  it by adjusting the the estate tax right  now it's only eleven million dollars and  up and we need to lower that so that we  can actually pay what we want in terms  of knowing that we want to invest in our  teachers and and the future of our  country in terms of how we came about  this legislation it's simple I've been  traveling the country meeting teachers  were working two jobs sometimes three  over ninety percent of our teachers are  coming out of their own pocket to pay  for school supplies no I'm talking about  a figure senator yep that's a terrific  figure  sure count so the data is very clear  that teachers are as compared to other  college graduates receiving 11% less and  pay across the country so we have data  that supports this point and that is the  equivalent of about thirteen thousand  five hundred dollars a year and thirteen  thousand five hundred dollars a year is  the equivalent of a year's worth of  mortgage payments the equivalent of a  year's worth of grocery bills the  equivalent of paying down student loans  for a year so it's a very significant  amount of money so senator what we  should remind bill that 50 million kids  are in public schools so this would  affect a huge group of families a couple  of criticism  you're likely to get first is the sort  of federalism argument that this is a  education should be a state issue yes  there's federal funds but it's better  when it's closer to the local level how  does that affect or what's your response  to that with respect to the pay raise I  think we can look at history to give us  a guide on this LBJ was probably the  last president who really put a  substantial investment in the public  education system of our country and we  reaped huge rewards for that and that's  how I think of this where the states  cannot act we should actually support  them and so specifically what we are  proposing is that there would be a  federal match three to one in terms of  what the states can give and then what  the federal government will give to  match state funding and I'll tell you  John it's important to understand this  is not a partisan issue you could look  at the teachers in Oklahoma and Arizona  so-called red states which have who have  demanded better equity around salaries  and we know it's because we have to  understand that the education of the  children of America is not a partisan  issue and it should be a nonpartisan  issue so there's obviously the equity  question on what people are getting paid  just for doing an honest day's work  going back to your original point though  in terms of improving the outcomes for  kids are you do you expect some kind of  benefit here in terms of recruiting new  people to come into the teaching  profession lutely and also and what  about outcomes that in terms of what the  studies have shown that might improve  from this bump up and pay you're  absolutely right it's going to be about  recruitment and retention we want that  that student who is excelling in  chemistry and biology that they will  pursue their passion of teaching  children instead of worrying about how  they're going to pay off their student  loans and going to some pharmaceutical  company we want that that that  mathematician who wants to teach our  children math we'll do that instead of  going to Wall Street because they got to  pay off their bills so there is gonna  humor tension piece of it and there's  another factor also which we have built  into this which is for the those school  districts that have the highest needs so  for example rural school districts where  there is a difficult it is difficult to  recruit people that we're actually going  to bump up the federal match to those  areas so we can create incentives  for these smart people these teachers to  go to areas where otherwise there are  not enough teachers to teach the  children of those communities all right  senator Harris that's all the time we  have thanks very much for being with us  I appreciate you thank you again soon  thank you thank you

